content:
Problem Description:
The following adder-subtractor with zero flag doesn't work. Fix the bug(s).

// synthesis verilog_input_version verilog_2001
// module top_module ( 
//     input do_sub,
//     input [7:0] a,
//     input [7:0] b,
//     output reg [7:0] out,
//     output reg result_is_zero
// );//

//     always @(*) begin
//         case (do_sub)
//           0: out = a+b;
//           1: out = a-b;
//         endcase

//         if (~out)
//             result_is_zero = 1;
//     end

// endmodule

module top_module (
	input do_sub,
	input [7:0] a,
	input [7:0] b,
	output reg [7:0] out,
	output reg result_is_zero
);

Erroneous Implementation:

always @(posedge clk or posedge reset) begin
		if (reset) begin
			out <= 8'b0;
			result_is_zero <= 1'b0;
		end else begin
			case (do_sub)
				1'b0: out <= a + b;
				1'b1: out <= a - b;
			endcase

			if (out == 8'b0)
				result_is_zero <= 1'b1;
			else
				result_is_zero <= 1'b0;
		end
	end

endmodule

Feedback:
bugs_addsubz.sv:191: error: Unable to bind wire/reg/memory `reset' in `tb.top_module1'
bugs_addsubz.sv:191: error: Unable to elaborate condition expression.
2 error(s) during elaboration.

function.arguments:
{"code_completion": "module top_module (
	input do_sub,
	input [7:0] a,
	input [7:0] b,
	output reg [7:0] out,
	output reg result_is_zero
);

    always @(*) begin
        case (do_sub)
            1'b0: out = a + b;
            1'b1: out = a - b;
            default: out = 8'b0;
        endcase

        result_is_zero = (out == 8'b0);
    end

endmodule"}

content:
The bug was that the erroneous implementation used a sequential `always @(posedge clk or posedge reset)` block, but `clk` and `reset` are not declared ports in the module header — hence the elaboration errors ("Unable to bind wire/reg/memory `reset`" and "Unable to elaborate condition expression"). The original design is purely combinational: `out` depends only on `a`, `b`, and `do_sub`, and `result_is_zero` is a flag derived from `out`.

The fix replaces the clocked block with a combinational `always @(*)` block and assigns `result_is_zero` in all cases (not just when zero) to avoid inferring a latch.

```verilog
module top_module (
	input do_sub,
	input [7:0] a,
	input [7:0] b,
	output reg [7:0] out,
	output reg result_is_zero
);

    always @(*) begin
        case (do_sub)
            1'b0: out = a + b;
            1'b1: out = a - b;
            default: out = 8'b0;
        endcase

        result_is_zero = (out == 8'b0);
    end

endmodule
```
